	\section{Introduction}
	
	Japanese yokai traditions are deeply spatial. In classic folklore scholarship, supernatural beings, phenomena, and anomalies are not only narrative motifs but also geographical phenomena. Water-related beings such as kappa are expected to appear near rivers, ponds, and irrigation systems; mountain beings such as tengu or yama-no-kami are expected to appear in upland and forested settings; and boundary beings are often narrated at passes, roads, bridges, and village edges. Yanagita Kunio's work on yokai and his diffusion-oriented account of cultural distribution further suggest that cultural forms may radiate from centers and persist in older forms at the periphery \citep{yanagita1930kagyuko}.
	
	Testing such ideas at archive scale requires seeing the geographic structure of the data without losing the uncertainty of historical and folkloric metadata. The Nichibun Kaii-Yokai Densho Database is a major digital collection of Japanese yokai and uncanny folklore records \citep{nichibun_yokai_database}. It contains tens of thousands of entries with names, regional metadata, source references, and short summaries. However, the archive is primarily designed for keyword lookup and table browsing. A researcher can find records by name or region, but cannot directly ask where water-related records concentrate relative to hydrological features, whether mountain-associated categories are inland, or whether category distributions show center-periphery structure.
	
	We present an open-source geospatial visualization system for the Nichibun yokai archive. The system transforms cleaned text records into enriched geospatial features and serves them through an interactive MapLibre GL map. It supports three analytic views: individual points for close reading, heatmap density for concentration patterns, and terrain bands for comparison with coast and water proximity. The purpose is not to replace philological interpretation, but to create a reproducible exploratory instrument where hypotheses about landscape, ecology, and diffusion can be generated, inspected, and prepared for formal testing.
	
	\section{Related Work and Motivation}
	
	Folklore geography has long treated narrative distribution as evidence for regional ecology, historical transmission, and local ritual practice. Yokai studies frequently connect beings to landscape categories: rivers, mountains, fields, shorelines, roads, settlement edges, and sacred or liminal sites \citep{foster2015book}. Digital humanities work has made large cultural archives searchable, but search does not automatically make spatial structure visible. GIS methods can bridge this gap, yet proprietary map stacks and manually geocoded datasets can make reproduction difficult.
	
	The system described here is designed around FOSS4G principles. The pipeline uses Python, spaCy, GiNZA, GeoPandas, Shapely, PyProj, geopy, OpenStreetMap Nominatim, GADM, Natural Earth, Next.js, and MapLibre GL \citep{python,spacy,ginza,geopandas,shapely,pyproj,geopy,nominatim,gadm,naturalearth,nextjs,maplibre}. The web map uses OpenStreetMap raster tiles rather than proprietary basemaps \citep{osm}. The pipeline is script-based and writes intermediate JSON and CSV files so that researchers can inspect, replace, or audit each stage.
	
	Table~\ref{tab:foss-stack} separates open-source software components from open data sources and public services. This distinction matters for reproducibility: Natural Earth is public domain, GADM is freely available for academic and non-commercial use, and public Nominatim and OpenStreetMap tile services have operational usage policies rather than being bulk-computation infrastructure \citep{naturalearth_terms,gadm_data,nominatim_policy}.
	
