	\section{Data}
	
	The input dataset is \texttt{data/nichibun/nichibun\_cleaned.json}, containing 33,378 cleaned Nichibun records. Each record includes an identifier, yokai name, name reading, prefecture, region, major category, phenomenon description, summary, and embedding text field. The present experiment uses all 33,378 records.
	
	Category counts are highly imbalanced: Other has 19,084 records, followed by Kitsune (3,618), Snake/Dragon (2,335), Yurei (1,641), Kappa (1,412), Tanuki (1,268), Tengu (1,224), Oni (807), Yama-no-kami (722), Inugami (552), Neko (466), and Hitodama (249). This imbalance motivates reporting effect sizes and category-level uncertainty rather than interpreting statistical significance alone.
	
	\section{System Pipeline}
	
	\subsection{Prefecture-Centroid Georeferencing}
	
	The first stage assigns a coordinate to every record using its structured \texttt{prefecture} field. The script \texttt{scripts/analysis/geocode\_prefecture.py} defines all 47 Japanese prefectures in a built-in centroid dictionary and appends \texttt{\_lat}, \texttt{\_lng}, and \texttt{\_geo\_level = "prefecture"}. The output is \texttt{data/nichibun/nichibun\_georef.json}. On the current cleaned dataset, all 33,378 records received coordinates.
	
	\subsection{Place Mention and Terrain Term Extraction}
	
	The second stage, \texttt{scripts/analysis/extract\_place\_names.py}, reads the georeferenced JSON and applies GiNZA, an open-source Japanese NLP pipeline, to the \texttt{summary} field \citep{ginza}. It stores place entities as \texttt{\_place\_mentions} and rule-based topographic words as \texttt{\_terrain\_terms}. The rule-based terrain vocabulary includes terms for mountains, rivers, passes, coasts, marshes, forests, rice paddies, bridges, roads, caves, rocks, and waterfalls. The script uses \texttt{data/nichibun/ner\_cache.json} so that long runs can be resumed without repeating completed records.
	
	\subsection{Optional Nominatim Refinement}
	
	The third stage, \texttt{scripts/analysis/geocode\_nominatim.py}, refines extracted place mentions with OpenStreetMap Nominatim through geopy \citep{nominatim,geopy}. The implementation uses a dedicated user agent, rate-limits requests to one per second, caches results in \texttt{data/nichibun/nominatim\_cache.json}, and constrains queries to Japan. A prefecture consistency check rejects results whose returned prefecture differs from the record's original prefecture. Successful records are upgraded to \texttt{\_geo\_level = "municipality"} and receive refined coordinates plus a \texttt{\_geocoded\_place} field.
	
	The current paper does not report municipality-level coverage or outcomes. Because public Nominatim use is rate-limited and full-archive refinement is a long-running process, the reported experiment is intentionally restricted to the completed prefecture-resolution run. This prevents partial geocoding from being mistaken for a validated sampling frame.
	
	\subsection{Terrain Enrichment}
	
	The terrain enrichment script, \texttt{scripts/analysis/terrain\_enrich.py}, downloads and caches GADM v4.1 Japan level 2 administrative boundaries, Natural Earth river centerlines, Natural Earth lakes, and Natural Earth coastlines \citep{gadm,naturalearth}. It joins each point to a GADM level 2 administrative unit and computes nearest distances to combined river/lake features and coastline using a projected coordinate reference system. Each record receives \texttt{\_dist\_water\_km}, \texttt{\_dist\_coast\_km}, \texttt{\_admin2}, and \texttt{\_terrain\_class}.
	
	The terrain classifier is deliberately simple and auditable. Records within 10 km of coast are labeled \texttt{coastal}; inland records more than 50 km from coast and within 2 km of water are labeled \texttt{valley}; records with mountain-related terms are labeled \texttt{mountain}; lake, pond, or marsh terms near water are labeled \texttt{inland\_water}; and remaining records are labeled \texttt{plain}. In the present run, coast and water distances are still computed from prefecture-centroid coordinates, but the mountain and inland-water classes are supplied by extracted terrain terms. The result should therefore be read as text-aware metadata geography, not fine-grained event geography.
	
	\subsection{GeoJSON API and Map Interface}
	
	The Next.js API route \path{src/app/api/yokai-geo/route.ts} lazily loads \path{data/nichibun/nichibun_enriched.json} into memory and serves a GeoJSON FeatureCollection. It supports filtering by category, geocoding level, terrain class, limit, and whether summaries should be included.
	
	The map interface at \texttt{/map} uses MapLibre GL with OpenStreetMap tiles \citep{maplibre,osm}. It supports three tabs: Points, Density, and Terrain. Points shows individual records colored by major category with popup summaries. Density uses a MapLibre heatmap layer. Terrain colors records by coast and water proximity and terrain class. For performance, if a filtered point set exceeds 5,000 records and the zoom is below 7, the Points and Terrain views display a deterministic 10\% sample; the Density view keeps the full filtered set because heatmap aggregation is handled client-side by MapLibre.
	
	\subsection{Analytical Views}
	
	The interface is organized around the folklore-geographic questions that motivate the system. The point view displays individual records colored by yokai category and links map locations back to names, prefectures, summaries, terrain class, and geocoding level. This view supports close reading of local clusters and anomalous records, such as a mountain-associated category appearing in an apparently coastal setting. The density view renders category-filtered archive density with a MapLibre heatmap layer, making broad concentration patterns easier to inspect than in a table of prefectures. The terrain view colors records by terrain class and distance filters, allowing researchers to compare category distributions against coast and water proximity.
	
	These views are intentionally exploratory. They are designed to make terrain-based and diffusion-based hypotheses inspectable before stronger statistical modeling is attempted. The system therefore preserves the resolution of each record through \texttt{\_geo\_level} and reports centroid-bias diagnostics in the analysis outputs. In the present experiment, all coordinates remain at prefecture-centroid resolution, so the interface should be read as a reproducible baseline for archive-scale spatial reasoning rather than as a final map of exact narrative locations.
	
	\section{Experiments}
	
	\subsection{Reproducible Setup}
	
	Experiments are implemented in \texttt{scripts/analysis/yokai\_geo\_experiments.py}. The script reads \texttt{data/nichibun/nichibun\_enriched.json} and writes outputs to \texttt{analysis/yokai\_geo/}: an experiment summary JSON file, category-terrain contingency tables, distance summaries, prefecture counts, and figures. The current run used prefecture centroids for all records. This resolution is sufficient to validate the data-processing and analysis pipeline and reveal coarse regional structure, but it cannot resolve local river, shrine, village, or pass-level relationships.
	
	\subsection{Terrain Distribution}
	
	The enrichment stage classified the 33,378 records as shown in Table~\ref{tab:terrain-counts}. This distribution partly reflects the use of prefecture centroids. Coastal prefectural capitals can make records from large inland prefectures appear more coastal than they would under municipality-level or locality-level geocoding. The result is therefore best treated as a diagnostic of pipeline behavior and geocoding uncertainty.
	
	A direct centroid-bias diagnostic confirms this issue: among the 47 prefecture centroids, 28 are classified as coastal, 17 as plain, and 2 as valley. Since every record in a prefecture inherits the same centroid coordinates in the reported run, each prefecture contributes a single terrain class regardless of within-prefecture topographic diversity. This is precisely why the system keeps \texttt{\_geo\_level} in every record and why the paper reports the experiment as a prefecture-centroid baseline.
	
