	\subsection{Category by Terrain}
	
	The category-terrain contingency table shows a statistically significant association between major category and the five non-empty text-aware terrain classes: $\chi^2=2090.40$, df = 44, $p<10^{-300}$, Cramer's $V=0.125$. The p-value is small because the dataset is large, while the effect size remains modest. The correct interpretation is therefore that category and coarse terrain class are not independent in the present representation, but the magnitude of association is not large enough to support deterministic ecological claims.
	
	Some category-level tendencies are visible in the contingency table and Figure~\ref{fig:terrain-proportions}. Kappa records are 59.2\% coastal under the centroid model, while Yama-no-kami records are 43.4\% mountain and Tengu records are 25.2\% mountain. Kitsune records are comparatively more plain-associated (41.4\%) and less coastal than expected. These are exploratory signals rather than final ecological findings because coordinates remain at prefecture resolution and terrain terms have not yet been manually validated.
	
	Standardized residuals clarify which cells drive the association. Yama-no-kami/mountain is much higher than expected (+24.05), Yama-no-kami/plain is lower than expected (-13.47), Tengu/mountain is higher than expected (+13.06), Snake/Dragon/inland-water is higher than expected (+12.78), and Kitsune/plain is higher than expected (+11.96). Excluding the large Other category increases the effect size from Cramer's $V=0.125$ to $V=0.173$ over 14,294 non-Other records. This sensitivity analysis indicates that the association is not only an artifact of the Other class, but remains best interpreted as a diagnostic pattern rather than a completed ecological test.
	
